{"name":"Hooked Mimosa","hex":"#ffc9c4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hooked","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of hook"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a sharp curve at the end; resembling a hook.","Addicted; unable to resist or cease doing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hooked"},{"term":"mimosa","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant belonging to the genus Mimosa usually found in tropical climates, their leaves are usually prickly and sensitive to touch or light, and have small white or pink flowers.","Certain plants of the genus Albizia (silk tree, pink siris)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mimosa"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mimosa","blocks":[{"text":"Mimosa is a genus of about 600 species of herbs and shrubs, in the mimosoid clade of the legume family Fabaceae. Species are native to the Americas, from North Dakota to northern Argentina, and to eastern Africa as well as the Indian subcontinent and Indochina. The generic name is derived from the Greek word μῖμος (mimos), 'actor' or 'mime', and the feminine suffix -osa, 'resembling', suggesting its 'sensitive leaves' which seem to 'mimic conscious life'."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Mimosa_flowers_01.jpg/330px-Mimosa_flowers_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mimosa","wikidata":"Q160110"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Mimosa-pudica-flower.jpg/500px-Mimosa-pudica-flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Mimosa-pudica-flower.jpg/1280px-Mimosa-pudica-flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Mimosa-pudica-flower.jpg